  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Storms have weakened almost completely. Expect \nthe trend to continue before it totally dissipates shortly. \n\nAs expected, the line of storms (which were more tame than\nexpected) has just about disappeared into the ether. Another\ngust to 25 is possible at JST in the next few minutes, but that\nwill be it. No more instability to the SE of the line. \n\nThe cold front and much drier air are not quite into PA at 815\nPM, but BUF and DKK (Dunkirk, NY) have dewpoints in the 50s,\nnow. Expect that to continue sliding this way. By morning, the\nnorthern mtns may have dewpoints in the upper 40s, and even the\nfar srn tier will have 55-60F Tds. Little fog is expected as the\ndry airmass moves in, but some of the northern valleys will\nprobably have a little fog as the get colder than the\nriver/creek water temps. \n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A taste of fall to start the workweek will be \nfollowed by increasing humidity and chances for rain for the \nsecond half of the week.\n\nDewpoints will fall rapidly behind the cold front, from near 70\nthis afternoon to the 40s and 50s by early Monday. The northern\ntier will see some low stratus downwind of the Great Lakes on \nMonday in NW flow. Otherwise, expect clear skies and a pleasant \nSeptember afternoon across much of central PA on Monday. With \nhigh pressure building overhead, radiational cooling will be \neffective Monday night bringing temperatures down into the 40s \nfor much of central PA with patchy river valley fog possible.\nValley fog will be possible again Tuesday night.\n\nReturn flow on the back side of the high pressure system moving\noffshore will bring increasing humidity for the middle and\nlatter part of the week. A slight chance for precip returns to\nthe forecast by mid week, with increasing chances for rain\nby early in the following week as the upper level pattern\nbecomes more amplified.",
